= Staal and the Banners

Defense Minister Staal has founded the *Reichskuratorium für Jugendertüchtigung* (Commonwealth Institute to Promote Youth Fitness), a program designed to have Commonwealth Army officers teach “military sports” to young workers. Staal aims to secure the participation of the Banners in this initiative, hoping it will serve as a counterweight to the <span style="color: #7A3C00;">**Loyalists**</span> and advance his broader strategy of integrating paramilitary groups into the state. Preparing youth for future military service has long been part of his rearmament agenda. The program will be led by General Stülpnagel, an advisor of his.

Staal has begun talks with <span style="color: #c00000;">Reformist</span> youth organizations, who have in turn informed the <span style="color: #c00000;">**Marchers**</span> leadership. While the Banners and the Greater Worker's Guild support participation, the <span style="color: #c00000;">**Marchers**</span> leadership is wary. They fear that joining the initiative is a thinly veiled attempt toward rearmament and would tie them too closely to the unpopular [+ chancellor +] government, and instead seek to maintain total opposition.

the Banners Chairman Karl Höltermann has put forward the most compelling argument in favor: without military training—especially for its officers—the Banners cannot hope to defend itself effectively against the <span style="color: #7A3C00;">**Loyalists**</span>.

- @yay
- @wait
- @nay

@yay
title: Approve the participation of the Banners and our youth organizations in the *Reichskuratorium*.
unavailable-subtitle: The party is too distrustful of Staal to allow this.
choose-if: staal_marcher_support >= 4 or knight_strength >= center_strength
on-arrival: staal_marcher_support += 3; staal_marcher_r += 1; banner_militancy += 0.06; banner_strength += 200; commons_marcher -= 4; disfavored_marcher -= 4; nationalism += 10

The Banners is now officially participating in the Commonwealth Institute to Promote Youth Fitness. Their board of trustees, with our input, has swiftly established infantry and naval training schools across the country. the Banners youth are the primary recipients of specialized instruction from former and decommissioned Commonwealth Army officers, and we have secured considerable influence within the institute’s leadership.

This program has strengthened the Banners and improved our relations with Staal. However, our collaboration with an initiative authorized by the deeply unpopular [+ chancellor +] government has cost us support—many have defected to the <span style="color: #700000;">**Collectivists**</span>.

@wait
title: Persuade the Banners to remain non-committal for now, at least until the next election.
on-arrival: staal_marcher_support += 1; staal_banner_wait = 1; banner_strength += 50; banner_militancy += 0.02

We have secured a commitment from the Banners leadership not to make any official decision regarding participation in the *Reichskuratorium* before the upcoming elections—a compromise between the party factions. However, some local members may still choose to enroll in the training programs on their own initiative.

@nay
title: Force the Banners to reject this offer, and persuade them to cut off communications with the general.
on-arrival: staal_marcher_r -= 1; staal_marcher_support -= 2; labor_dissent += 10; knight_dissent += 10

Our explicit rejection of the Banners participation in the program has caused discontent within both the Greater Worker's Guild and the Banners, who have nonetheless reluctantly yielded to the party leadership’s decision. As a <span style="color: #c00000;">Reformist</span> party, we cannot lend our support to what is, in essence, a thinly veiled rearmament scheme.